00:28:20,820 --> 00:28:23,540
I mean, yeah, that is it.

501
00:28:23,540 --> 00:28:24,380
He's right!

502
00:28:25,420 --> 00:28:27,140
Can you put an airbud quote in here?

503
00:28:27,140 --> 00:28:28,540
But you won't find anything in there

504
00:28:28,540 --> 00:28:29,860
says a dog can't play.

505
00:28:29,860 --> 00:28:30,700
He's right!

506
00:28:30,700 --> 00:28:33,620
Ain't no rule says the dog can't play basketball.

507
00:28:33,620 --> 00:28:34,980
This is a joke, kiddie.

508
00:28:34,980 --> 00:28:37,020
Dogs don't play basketball.

509
00:28:37,020 --> 00:28:38,140
But no, seriously, Lohanna,

510
00:28:38,140 --> 00:28:40,100
could there hypothetically be a dog mayor?

511
00:28:40,100 --> 00:28:41,900
There are dog mayors, they're just not,

512
00:28:41,900 --> 00:28:44,740
they're not part of the official election.

513
00:28:44,740 --> 00:28:45,580
Are they on the ballot?

514
00:28:45,580 --> 00:28:47,940
Do you know sometimes dog mayors have duties

515
00:28:47,940 --> 00:28:50,980
like they'll show up to a business opening

516
00:28:50,980 --> 00:28:53,420
or so they're available for that sort of thing?

517
00:28:53,420 --> 00:28:54,980
Wait, wait, are you, I actually don't know this.

518
00:28:54,980 --> 00:28:56,780
Are there actually dog mayors?

519
00:28:56,780 --> 00:28:57,620
I did not know this.

520
00:28:57,620 --> 00:29:00,740
There are, okay, there are like quote unquote,

521
00:29:00,740 --> 00:29:01,580
I mean there are elections,

522
00:29:01,580 --> 00:29:03,260
there are elections for dog mayors,

523
00:29:03,260 --> 00:29:06,340
but it's not going to be on the official ballot.

524
00:29:06,340 --> 00:29:08,100
Do you ever wonder if the dogs have mayors

525
00:29:08,100 --> 00:29:09,420
kind of like in their own government?

526
00:29:09,420 --> 00:29:10,780
I think about that a lot.

527
00:29:10,780 --> 00:29:12,380
Yeah, yeah.

528
00:29:12,380 --> 00:29:15,100
All right, Nick, so we've got like the mayor system,

529
00:29:15,100 --> 00:29:17,400
We've got the city councilor system,

530
00:29:17,400 --> 00:29:19,460
we've got sort of the combination of the two,

531
00:29:19,460 --> 00:29:23,240
but some municipalities have totally different systems.

532
00:29:23,240 --> 00:29:24,980
You could have commissioners.

533
00:29:24,980 --> 00:29:26,580
This is a small group of people

534
00:29:26,580 --> 00:29:29,360
who kind of act like a city council.

535
00:29:29,360 --> 00:29:32,460
This is not a super common form of government, it's very old.

536
00:29:32,460 --> 00:29:36,100
You could have one of the forms of town meeting government

537
00:29:36,100 --> 00:29:38,560
where people directly vote for stuff

538
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:40,360
or they vote for a bunch of people

539
00:29:40,360 --> 00:29:41,940
who will vote on their behalf.

540
00:29:41,940 --> 00:29:43,600
And then there's a select board

541
00:29:43,600 --> 00:29:44,940
who makes sure stuff gets done.

542
00:29:44,940 --> 00:29:46,140
Like Widows Bay.

543
00:29:46,140 --> 00:29:49,780
Just like Widows Bay, which among its many other mysteries

544
00:29:49,780 --> 00:29:52,220
also has an elected executive mayor.

545
00:29:52,220 --> 00:29:54,620
And then like I said, there are people

546
00:29:54,620 --> 00:29:57,580
at the super local level doing the same basic stuff

547
00:29:57,580 --> 00:29:59,580
that happens at the county level,

548
00:29:59,580 --> 00:30:03,040
except they are doing it for your city or town.

549
00:30:03,040 --> 00:30:05,000
And then there's one last piece

550
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:08,180
to the local puzzle nick, districts.

551
00:30:08,180 --> 00:30:10,180
["Districts"]

552
00:30:13,040 --> 00:30:14,580
Like a school district.

553
00:30:14,580 --> 00:30:16,140
Yeah, just like a school district.

554
00:30:16,140 --> 00:30:18,260
There are also things called special districts

555
00:30:18,260 --> 00:30:21,620
that take care of super focused services

556
00:30:21,620 --> 00:30:25,060
that are not limited to a city's boundary

557
00:30:25,060 --> 00:30:26,580
or a county's boundary.

558
00:30:26,580 --> 00:30:28,740
There can be water districts,

559
00:30:28,740 --> 00:30:32,940
fire protection districts, mosquito control districts.

560
00:30:32,940 --> 00:30:34,380
Mosquito control.

561
00:30:34,380 --> 00:30:35,380
You heard me right.

562
00:30:35,380 --> 00:30:37,140
Which I actually really like as an example

563
00:30:37,140 --> 00:30:40,860
because the problem or concern is right there in the name.

564
00:30:40,860 --> 00:30:43,420
There's some area within a state

565
00:30:43,420 --> 00:30:46,100
or within a town that needs help with mosquitoes

566
00:30:46,100 --> 00:30:48,100
so they made a special district

567
00:30:48,100 --> 00:30:51,660
and created a board of elected officials to handle it.

568
00:30:51,660 --> 00:30:56,020
Nick, these special district boards might determine

569
00:30:56,020 --> 00:30:57,820
what you pay for electricity

570
00:30:57,820 --> 00:31:01,180
or how long it takes for a fire engine to get to your street

571
00:31:01,180 --> 00:31:04,940
or whether or not your library is open on Sundays.

572
00:31:04,940 --> 00:31:07,740
Or with school districts, as I will know,

573
00:31:07,740 --> 00:31:09,660
how are your kids graded in school?

574
00:31:09,660 --> 00:31:11,180
What kinds of activities can they do?

575
00:31:11,180 --> 00:31:13,220
Do you have to pay for those activities?

576
00:31:13,220 --> 00:31:15,380
what's the school district budget?

577
00:31:15,380 --> 00:31:17,780
I think about this one a lot.

578
00:31:17,780 --> 00:31:20,660
It is a big one for both parents

579
00:31:20,660 --> 00:31:23,080
and I think a lot of non-parents out there alike.

580
00:31:23,080 --> 00:31:26,660
People really care about how education is run.

581
00:31:26,660 --> 00:31:27,780
By the way, school districts,

582
00:31:27,780 --> 00:31:30,540
those are not the same thing as special districts

583
00:31:30,540 --> 00:31:33,780
according to the Census Bureau, their own thing.

584
00:31:33,780 --> 00:31:35,860
And they're weird, right?

585
00:31:35,860 --> 00:31:36,860
Weird how?

586
00:31:36,860 --> 00:31:39,020
Well, like you have given me the example

587
00:31:39,020 --> 00:31:43,520
of the fact that your kids went to a school

588
00:31:43,520 --> 00:31:45,660
that was in a different district

589
00:31:45,660 --> 00:31:47,880
than the houses across the street from that school.

590
00:31:47,880 --> 00:31:48,720
Yeah, yeah.

591
00:31:48,720 --> 00:31:50,300
I once lived somewhere where you could stand outside

592
00:31:50,300 --> 00:31:53,220
in your yard and watch kids playing at school,

593
00:31:53,220 --> 00:31:56,000
and you could not be allowed to send your kids

594
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:58,400
to that school, because it was in a different district.

595
00:31:58,400 --> 00:31:59,240
Yeah.

596
00:31:59,240 --> 00:32:02,060
And while we were talking about school districts,

597
00:32:02,060 --> 00:32:03,980
it is my understanding that in the United States,

598
00:32:03,980 --> 00:32:07,740
they're pretty segregated racially and economically.

599
00:32:07,740 --> 00:32:09,940
Yeah, they are.

600
00:32:09,940 --> 00:32:12,380
And school board members, Nick,

601
00:32:12,380 --> 00:32:17,380
are usually deciding the attendance zone boundary.

602
00:32:17,420 --> 00:32:18,940
So they are deciding, you know,

603
00:32:18,940 --> 00:32:19,780
based on your address,

604
00:32:19,780 --> 00:32:21,820
whether you get to go to one school or another,

605
00:32:21,820 --> 00:32:25,020
or they are hiring the people who make those decisions.

606
00:32:25,020 --> 00:32:26,940
And, you know, to your point,

607
00:32:26,940 --> 00:32:29,500
a lot of what kids get to learn,

608
00:32:29,500 --> 00:32:32,020
where the funds go, all of that,

609
00:32:32,020 --> 00:32:35,980
you, the voter, are often in charge of picking

610
00:32:35,980 --> 00:32:39,060
who gets to make those decisions.

611
00:32:39,060 --> 00:32:40,900
And you know, Nick, at the beginning of this episode,

612
00:32:40,900 --> 00:32:43,340
I said that local elections are special

613
00:32:43,340 --> 00:32:45,060
because you're more likely to know

614
00:32:45,060 --> 00:32:48,420
and interact with the person you're voting for.

615
00:32:48,420 --> 00:32:51,060
They're also special because you're voting for people

616
00:32:51,060 --> 00:32:53,720
who make very specific decisions

617
00:32:53,720 --> 00:32:58,720
that you are just more likely to see and experience

618
00:32:58,900 --> 00:33:01,060
and to understand for that matter.

619
00:33:01,060 --> 00:33:03,540
A federal candidate might campaign on, you know,

620
00:33:03,540 --> 00:33:04,940
supporting local business,

621
00:33:04,940 --> 00:33:09,320
but a local candidate who has a plan for creating

622
00:33:09,320 --> 00:33:14,320
like a micro loan program to start businesses in your town,

623
00:33:14,560 --> 00:33:18,660
that is probably gonna be way more tangible for you.

624
00:33:18,660 --> 00:33:21,660
["The Last Ten Years"]

625
00:33:27,660 --> 00:33:29,820
We have said it dozens of times on our show

626
00:33:29,820 --> 00:33:32,300
over the last 10 years about how a stop sign

627
00:33:32,300 --> 00:33:33,500
at the end of your block is, you know,

628
00:33:33,500 --> 00:33:35,440
more important than who the president is,

629
00:33:35,440 --> 00:33:38,480
but this is really making that abundantly clear.

630
00:33:38,480 --> 00:33:41,520
Like again, I have interacted with all of these positions

631
00:33:41,520 --> 00:33:43,320
that we have just talked about.

632
00:33:43,320 --> 00:33:46,480
I was not always aware that I could vote for them, however.

633
00:33:47,480 --> 00:33:49,460
Yeah, I mean, for all of our talk about the importance

634
00:33:49,460 --> 00:33:52,480
of local power and local participation, you know,

635
00:33:52,480 --> 00:33:54,560
the role voters play in the place

636
00:33:54,560 --> 00:33:56,960
where they specifically live and, you know,

637
00:33:56,960 --> 00:34:00,400
the stuff that happens to and around them on a daily basis,

638
00:34:00,400 --> 00:34:03,360
to your point, for all of those years of insisting

639
00:34:03,360 --> 00:34:05,640
that it is so true that that's so important.

640
00:34:05,640 --> 00:34:07,600
Researching this episode, Nick,

641
00:34:07,600 --> 00:34:10,600
still hit me like this avalanche of proof.

642
00:34:10,600 --> 00:34:13,240
I was like, oh, I was right.

643
00:34:14,240 --> 00:34:16,240
You know, so if you're out there

644
00:34:16,240 --> 00:34:19,480
having trouble seeing democracy in action,

645
00:34:19,480 --> 00:34:22,320
you just might need to zoom in a little bit.

646
00:34:22,320 --> 00:34:24,600
Yeah, it's so much closer than I think

647
00:34:24,600 --> 00:34:26,640
a lot of us might realize.

648
00:34:26,640 --> 00:34:30,360
So, if you have not voted yet, remember,

649
00:34:30,360 --> 00:34:32,680
you can be familiar with your ballot

650
00:34:32,680 --> 00:34:35,460
before you ever get to your polling place.

651
00:34:35,460 --> 00:34:38,980
Look up that sample ballot, look up the candidates,

652
00:34:38,980 --> 00:34:40,780
and if you want, you can do what I do.

653
00:34:40,780 --> 00:34:43,380
You can make a list of your voting choices,

654
00:34:43,380 --> 00:34:45,160
like the candidates you're gonna vote for,

655
00:34:45,160 --> 00:34:47,880
for each office before you head out to vote.

656
00:34:47,880 --> 00:34:49,900
Bring that list into the booth.

657
00:34:49,900 --> 00:34:52,600
And while I didn't cover every single local office,

658
00:34:52,600 --> 00:34:56,760
people will have the chance to vote for this year.

659
00:34:56,760 --> 00:35:00,060
I do hope one thing is clear, voting,

660
00:35:00,060 --> 00:35:03,700
including what and who you are voting for

661
00:35:03,700 --> 00:35:07,240
decides your American life.

662
00:35:07,240 --> 00:35:10,500
And you can leave that choice to others.

663
00:35:10,500 --> 00:35:12,980
Or you can make it yourself.

